	(text "Surge protection"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 49.276 135.636 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
		)
		(uuid "00660170-85df-4a13-832d-8c5a9636b5b6")
	)
	(text "TVS diode on CANH/CANL at the connector. \nProtects the transceiver from ESD and voltage spikes \ncoming in off the cable.\nPlaced at J1 so surges are clamped before they reach U1."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 48.26 162.56 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.635 0.635)
			)
		)
		(uuid "0aeeed9c-6d61-409b-9945-8cbf3bc18aa6")
	)
	(text "5V -> 3V3 LDO"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 39.116 51.308 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
		)
		(uuid "51edd3ff-969b-40bd-a76f-34a35ef36420")
	)
	(text "Ali Cheema"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 230.378 163.576 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "76b4a2e9-25bc-49c2-bbd8-6138fac64694")
	)
	(text "You have been given a task to connect an LED to a CAN bus network.\n\nCAN is a popular communication protocol used in the automotive industry. \nThe CAN bus sends data instructions along a differential pair to other devices. \nA differential pair is a pair of wires starting at a \"middle\" or \"resting\" voltage level, and when a data bit is sent, one wire's voltage increases by a certain amount, and the other wire decreases in voltage by the same amount.\nIn the case of CAN 5V, the wires rest at 2.5V which is 'logic 0', and data representing a 'logic 1' causes CANH to reach ~3.5V while CANL drops to ~1.5V.\n\nThe MCP25625 offers a CAN transceiver and CAN controller.\nThe CAN transceiver collects data from the CAN lines.\nThe CAN controller reads the electrical signal that the transceiver collected and determines whether a proper CAN instruction has been received.\nThe MCP25625 then sends this instruction to a microcontroller using another communication protocol: 4-wire SPI.\n\nThe STM32G030F6Px is a microcontroller that will toggle the LED.\n\nThe LED is a very basic LED, no internal state.\n\nYour task is to connect the components below to the external signals provided (5V, GND, CANH, CANL), and connect the LED to the microcontroller.\nYou will need to find the datasheets online for the components and learn how they must be wired up in order to function.\nYou will need to find and implement what the industry uses for basic signal protection such as decoupling and surge protection.\n\nDo not worry about the OSC pins on the MCP25625. The quartz oscillator is technically required, but not for this intro task."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 12.954 26.162 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "927bf91b-39c9-472b-b6da-a2f9f3d4b9c2")
	)
	(text "Assuming this is the end node \nof the can bus, \nif used as a mid-bus nods \nthen DNP the resistor"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 85.09 150.114 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.635 0.635)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b0d35e3c-c4df-4f47-8a05-9e9e93368622")
	)
	(text "U1 decoupling: VIO and VDD"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 73.406 51.054 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
		)
		(uuid "c3cd3334-ea12-4f4e-9aee-a8f65f06785f")
	)
